(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-06-risk-exposure-to-disease-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 6: Risk: Exposure to Disease Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following statements is not correct for both prospective and retrospective cohort studies?","choices":["They measure incidence of disease directly.","They allow assessment of possible associations between exposure and many diseases.","They allow investigators to decide beforehand what data to collect.","They avoid bias that might occur if measurement of exposure is made after the outcome of interest is known."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Retrospective cohort studies do not allow investigators the luxury of deciding what data to collect. They can only choose from the available data collected at a time before the study originated. Also, these data, which are often collected for clinical purposes, may not have been collected systematically and in the same way. Answers A, B, and D are correct for both prospective and retrospective cohort studies. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.1"},{"q":"A study was done examining the relationship of smoking, stroke, and age. The 12-year incidence per 1,000 persons (absolute risk) of stroke according to age and smoking status was:\n\n45–49: Nonsmokers 7.4, Smokers 29.7; 65–69: Nonsmokers 80.2, Smokers 110.4.\n\nWhich of the following statements about the study results is incorrect?","choices":["To calculate population-attributable risk of smoking among people in their 60s, additional data are needed.","More cases of stroke due to smoking occurred in people in their 60s than in their 40s.","When relative risk is calculated, the results reflect information about the incidence in exposed and unexposed persons, whereas the results for attributable risk do not.","The calculated relative risk is a stronger argument for smoking as cause of stroke for persons in their 40s than the calculated risk for persons in their 60s.","Depending on the question asked, age could be considered as either a confounding variable or an effect modifier in the study."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Relative risk gives no information about incidence, whereas AR does, so the statement in C is incorrect. The other answers are all correct. To calculate population AR, one must know the prevalence of a risk factor in the population, which is not given in the question. The incidence of stroke among smokers was higher in the 60s than in the 40s. The RR of stroke among smokers in the 40s was 4.0, compared to 1.4 in the 60s. Stronger relative risks are better evidence for a causal relationship than weaker ones. In the analysis of this study, age could be treated as either a confounding variable and controlled for or, as in the information presented, as an effect modifier showing that the effect of smoking varies by age. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.4"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the absolute risk of DVT in women who do not have the mutation and do not take OCs?","choices":["0.8/10,000/yr","1.3/10,000/yr","2.2/10,000/yr","9.5/10,000/yr","25.5/10,000/yr"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Absolute risk is another term for incidence. DVT is a rare event in most women, and the incidence in this study was 0.8/10,000/yr among women who neither had the mutation nor took OCs. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.5"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the attributable risk of DVT for women taking OCs who do not carry the mutation for factor V Leiden compared to those not taking OCs and not carrying the mutation?","choices":["0.8/10,000/yr","1.3/10,000/yr","2.2/10,000/yr","9.5/10,000/yr","25.5/10,000/yr"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A good way to organize your thinking for questions 6.6–6.10 is to create a 2 × 2 table of the incidence of DVT according to OC use (present/absent) and factor V Leiden (present/absent). Incidence (per 10,000/yr) of DVT According to OC Use and Mutation for Factor V Leiden Present: OC use present 28.5, OC use absent 5.7; Absent: OC use present 3.0, OC use absent 0.8. The table shows that among women who do not carry the factor V Leiden mutation the AR of DVT for taking OC compared to those who do not take OC is 3.0/10,000 – 0.8/10,000 = 2.2/10,000. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.6"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the attributable risk of DVT for women taking OCs who carry factor V Leiden compared to women on OCs but not carrying the mutation?","choices":["0.8/10,000/yr","1.3/10,000/yr","2.2/10,000/yr","9.5/10,000/yr","25.5/10,000/yr"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct. The AR of DVT among OC users who also carry factor V Leiden is substantial when compared to most women using OC, 28.5/10,000 – 3.0/10,000 = 25.5/10,000. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.7"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nIn a population with 100,000 white women, all of whom take OCs, what is the population-attributable risk for DVT in women who are heterozygous for factor V Leiden?","choices":["0.8/10,000/yr","1.3/10,000/yr","2.2/10,000/yr","9.5/10,000/yr","25.5/10,000/yr"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Population attributable risk = attributable risk (25.5/10,000 women/yr) × prevalence of factor V Leiden (0.05) = 1.3/10,000 women/yr. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.8"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the relative risk of DVT in women taking OCs and heterozygous for factor V Leiden compared to women who take OCs but do not carry the mutation?","choices":["3.8","7.1","9.5","28.5","35.6"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Relative risk is calculated by dividing the incidence of DVT among women carrying the factor V Leiden mutation and using OCs by the incidence of DVT in women who take OCs but do not carry the mutation (28.5/10,000/yr ÷ 3.0/10,000 = 9.5). — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.9"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the relative risk of DVT in women taking OCs and without the mutation compared to women without the mutation who are not taking OCs?","choices":["3.8","7.1","9.5","28.5","35.6"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Among women without the mutation, the relative risk for DVT and using OCs is calculated by dividing the incidence among those taking OCs by the incidence among those not using OCs (3.0/10,000/yr ÷ 0.8/10,000/yr = 3.8). — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.10"}]};
